The git history is grafted. Let me look at the actual vulnerable code more carefully. The issue is in `md_is_link_title`:

```c
while(line_index < n_lines) {
    OFF line_end = lines[line_index].end;

    while(off < line_end) {
        if(CH(off) == _T('\\')  &&  off+1 < ctx->size  &&  (ISPUNCT(off+1) || ISNEWLINE(off+1))) {
            off++;
        } else if(CH(off) == closer_char) {
            ...
        } else if(closer_char == _T(')')  &&  CH(off) == _T('(')) {
            return FALSE;
        }
        off++;
    }
    line_index++;
}
```

The issue: when `off` is at the end of a line, `off` is set to `lines[line_index].end`. Then `line_index++` moves to the next line. But `off` is NOT reset to `lines[line_index].beg` in the loop! So when we enter the next iteration, `off` is still at the previous line's end, which could be beyond the current line's `end`, causing `CH(off)` to read out of bounds.

Wait, let me re-read. Actually, `off` starts at `beg` which is within the first line. The whitespace skipping at the top handles the first line break. But inside the main loop, after finishing a line, `off` is at `lines[line_index].end` (the end of the current line). Then `line_index++` moves to the next line, but `off` is NOT reset to `lines[line_index].beg`. So in the next iteration, `off` could be greater than `line_end = lines[line_index].end` if the next line is shorter, and the inner `while(off < line_end)` loop would be skipped, but then `line_index++` again... 

Wait, actually the inner loop `while(off < line_end)` would be skipped if `off >= line_end`. Then `line_index++` and the outer loop continues. So `off` never gets reset to the beginning of the new line. This means the title content is never actually scanned across lines properly, and `off` stays at the old line's end.

But the actual heap-buffer-overflow: Let me think about how `off` could go out of bounds. The `CH(off)` macro reads `ctx->text[off]`. If `off` is beyond `ctx->size`, that's an OOB read.

Let me look at the `CH` macro and understand the buffer.

